::ENG4_U06_L02_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Presentation excerpt: We need clear evidence, clear reasoning, and clear action. Which device is used? {
=A.  Repetition and parallel structure.#Correct. Clear is repeated and the phrase pattern is balanced.
~B.  Unrelated evidence.#Incorrect. The sentence uses a rhetorical structure, not evidence.
~C.  Sentence fragment only.#Incorrect. The sentence is complete.
~D.  Source citation.#Incorrect. No source is cited.
}

::ENG4_U06_L02_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.R.12] Why is the repeated word clear effective in the excerpt? {
~A.  It distracts from the point.#Incorrect. The repetition supports the point.
=B.  It emphasizes the qualities the speaker wants the audience to remember.#Correct. Repetition makes the key idea memorable.
~C.  It replaces all evidence.#Incorrect. Rhetoric should not replace evidence.
~D.  It makes the claim unrelated.#Incorrect. The repetition reinforces the claim.
}

::ENG4_U06_L02_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Which use of rhetoric is weakest? {
~A.  A rhetorical question after evidence has been explained.#Incorrect. That can be effective.
~B.  Parallel structure that organizes three reasons.#Incorrect. That can strengthen organization.
=C.  A dramatic claim with no evidence or reasoning.#Correct. Rhetoric without evidence weakens argument quality.
~D.  Repetition that emphasizes a proven pattern.#Incorrect. That can support evidence.
}

::ENG4_U06_L02_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Which explanation uses academic vocabulary precisely? {
~A.  The speaker says words in a speech.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~B.  The speech is speechy and good.#Incorrect. This is not academic language.
~C.  The device is a thing.#Incorrect. This is vague.
=D.  The speaker's parallel structure emphasizes three related claims.#Correct. This uses parallel structure and emphasizes accurately.
}

::ENG4_U06_L02_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Which revision best uses rhetoric responsibly? {
=A.  Use parallel structure to summarize three evidence-based reasons.#Correct. This supports clear oral presentation.
~B.  Add a rhetorical question that ignores the evidence.#Incorrect. Rhetoric should connect to evidence.
~C.  Use dramatic wording to hide weak support.#Incorrect. This replaces reasoning with style.
~D.  Repeat unrelated words for effect.#Incorrect. Repetition should serve the message.
}
